/**
  @page guides-index General concepts
  @brief Conceptual guides to understanding Rodin's design and features.

  @tableofcontents

  @section guides-getting-started-index Getting started with Rodin

  New to Rodin? Start here. These guides take you from installation to
  solving your first PDE:

  - @subpage guides-getting-started-installation — Install Rodin and set up
    your development environment

  - @subpage guides-getting-started-first-steps — Learn the basic Rodin
    classes (@ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "Mesh",
    @ref Rodin::Variational::P1 "P1",
    @ref Rodin::Variational::TrialFunction "TrialFunction",
    @ref Rodin::Variational::TestFunction "TestFunction") and write your first
    program

  - @subpage guides-getting-started-first-problem — Solve a complete PDE
    (the Poisson equation) step by step

  - @subpage guides-getting-started-core-concepts — Deeper dive into meshes,
    finite element spaces, operators, and variational formulations

  @subsection guides-getting-started-prerequisites Prerequisites

  Before starting, you should have:
  - Basic knowledge of C++17/C++20 (templates, lambdas, auto)
  - Some familiarity with calculus and linear algebra
  - Familiarity with finite element methods is helpful but not required —
    the guides introduce the necessary theory
  - A Linux or macOS system with a C++20 compatible compiler (GCC 12+)

  @section guides-geometry-index Geometry

  The @ref Rodin::Geometry "Geometry" module handles meshes, polytopes,
  connectivity, and geometric transformations:

  - @subpage guides-meshes — Creating, loading, and querying meshes

  - @subpage guides-polytopes — Geometric elements (triangles, tetrahedra,
    etc.) and the reference element @f$ \hat{K} @f$

  - @subpage guides-connectivity — Topological incidence relations between
    polytopes of different dimensions

  @section guides-fem-index The finite element method

  Mathematical foundations and their realization in Rodin:

  - @subpage fem-ciarlet — Ciarlet's formal definition of a finite element:
    the triple @f$ (K, P, \Sigma) @f$

  - @subpage fem-spaces — Available finite element spaces (P0, P1, H1),
    vector-valued and complex-valued variants, and the
    @ref Rodin::Variational::GridFunction "GridFunction" class

  @section guides-variational-index Weak formulations and variational problems

  From continuous PDEs to discrete linear systems:

  - @subpage guides-variational — Defining problems using bilinear forms,
    linear forms, and boundary conditions

  - @subpage guides-weak-formulations — Deriving weak formulations from
    strong-form PDEs (Poisson, elasticity)

  - @subpage guides-boundary-conditions — Dirichlet, Neumann, periodic,
    and mixed boundary conditions

  - @subpage guides-solvers — Direct solvers (SparseLU, UMFPack, CHOLMOD),
    iterative solvers (CG, GMRES, BiCGSTAB), and the Newton solver

  @section guides-io-index I/O in Rodin

  Reading and writing meshes, grid functions, and simulation data:

  - @subpage guides-io — Supported formats (MFEM, MEDIT, HDF5, XDMF),
    ParaView visualization, and time-series output

  @section guides-getting-started-help Getting Help

  - Check the [GitHub repository](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin)
    for the latest code and issues
  - Browse the @ref examples-index "examples and tutorials" for common
    use cases
  - Consult the @ref notation-index "notation reference" for mathematical
    symbols used in the documentation

  */

